WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.340
Hi, I'm Nei Maldaner,

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.280
and this is module three of

3
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:04.820
OSWork.

4
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:05.880
In module two,

5
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:06.860
you saw chat,

6
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.660
Work, and Desktop,

7
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.560
three ways to work with AI.

8
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.520
Now the AI starts working in a

9
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.760
folder on your computer,

10
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.420
through the terminal.

11
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.740
It may sound like something for programmers,

12
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:19.160
but we'll go slowly,

13
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.060
one command at a time.

14
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.240
By the end of the module,

15
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.000
you'll open the terminal and know where

16
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.160
you are,

17
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.560
install Codex from the official source,

18
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.780
sign in with your account without leaving

19
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.460
any password in sight,

20
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.560
open the agent in the right folder,

21
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.980
ask first for a reading and then

22
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.140
for a small change,

23
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.340
and check what changed.

24
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.240
Denise and Lúcia are still with us.

25
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.180
Each lesson has a hands-on practice on

26
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.180
the computer, with training files.

27
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.120
When I ask,

28
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.940
pause the video and do it right

29
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:49.640
then.

30
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:50.140
From now on,

31
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.940
the AI works in a folder on

32
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:52.840
your computer.

33
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.600
And if you don't know which folder

34
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.500
you're in, it doesn't know either.

35
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.300
Many errors that look like AI failures

36
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.260
are just the wrong folder.

37
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.007
The terminal is a window where you

38
00:01:02.007 --> 00:01:03.507
type a command and read the answer,

39
00:01:03.507 --> 00:01:05.347
and it already comes with your computer.

40
00:01:05.347 --> 00:01:06.507
On the Mac,

41
00:01:06.507 --> 00:01:09.027
Lúcia pressed Cmd and Space,

42
00:01:09.027 --> 00:01:11.027
typed Terminal, and hit Enter.

43
00:01:11.027 --> 00:01:12.167
It took ten seconds.

44
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:14.907
On Linux, just look for Terminal in

45
00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:16.127
your list of apps.

46
00:01:16.127 --> 00:01:18.147
The commands in this course are written

47
00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:20.227
in Bash, the language of the terminal

48
00:01:20.227 --> 00:01:22.367
on Linux and macOS.

49
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:24.687
On Windows, they work inside WSL,

50
00:01:24.687 --> 00:01:26.967
which comes from Microsoft itself and opens

51
00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:27.467
as Ubuntu.

52
00:01:27.427 --> 00:01:29.767
The terminal that comes with Windows uses

53
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:30.867
a different language,

54
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:32.647
so don't paste the course commands into

55
00:01:32.647 --> 00:01:34.667
it without adapting them.

56
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:35.547
A school computer,

57
00:01:35.547 --> 00:01:37.907
with no administrator password?

58
00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:39.127
Don't force it:

59
00:01:39.127 --> 00:01:40.547
ask whoever manages it.

60
00:01:40.547 --> 00:01:41.847
When it opens,

61
00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:43.847
the terminal shows a short line ending

62
00:01:43.847 --> 00:01:45.507
in a dollar sign,

63
00:01:45.507 --> 00:01:46.927
or, on the Mac,

64
00:01:46.927 --> 00:01:47.967
a percent sign,

65
00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:49.467
with the cursor blinking.

66
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:51.927
That's the computer waiting for you.

67
00:01:51.927 --> 00:01:53.767
Denise opened the terminal for the first

68
00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:55.807
time and waited for something to happen.

69
00:01:55.807 --> 00:01:57.527
Nothing happened,

70
00:01:57.527 --> 00:01:59.087
because it was waiting for her.

71
00:01:59.087 --> 00:02:00.807
You type the command,

72
00:02:00.807 --> 00:02:02.553
press Enter, the answer shows up below,

73
00:02:02.553 --> 00:02:04.613
and the dollar sign comes back.

74
00:02:04.613 --> 00:02:06.633
It's always that back and forth.

75
00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:07.913
The first command is pwd.

76
00:02:07.913 --> 00:02:10.413
You know the school map with the

77
00:02:10.413 --> 00:02:11.973
“you are here” sticker?

78
00:02:12.123 --> 00:02:13.493
pwd plays that role:

79
00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:15.353
it answers with the full path of

80
00:02:15.353 --> 00:02:16.453
the folder you're in.

81
00:02:16.453 --> 00:02:17.973
Read it like an address.

82
00:02:17.973 --> 00:02:19.933
Each slash separates a folder,

83
00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:21.273
and the last one is where you

84
00:02:21.273 --> 00:02:22.013
are.

85
00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:25.833
Lúcia typed pwd and read slash Users

86
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:27.733
slash lucia:

87
00:02:27.953 --> 00:02:29.413
her personal folder.

88
00:02:29.413 --> 00:02:31.433
Nothing was created or deleted.

89
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:32.753
Once you know where you are,

90
00:02:32.753 --> 00:02:33.933
look at what's there.

91
00:02:33.933 --> 00:02:36.373
The ls command lists the folders and

92
00:02:36.373 --> 00:02:38.053
files in the current place.

93
00:02:38.053 --> 00:02:39.453
It shows what a program will see

94
00:02:39.453 --> 00:02:41.953
if it starts working from there.

95
00:02:42.413 --> 00:02:44.573
Denise opened Codex in the Downloads folder,

96
00:02:44.573 --> 00:02:46.913
while testing something for a colleague,

97
00:02:46.913 --> 00:02:48.933
and it couldn't see her project,

98
00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:50.693
which was in another folder.

99
00:02:50.693 --> 00:02:53.713
A pwd would have shown that beforehand.

100
00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:55.273
To change folders

101
00:02:55.273 --> 00:02:58.153
there's cd, which we'll leave for lesson

102
00:02:58.153 --> 00:02:58.833
sixteen.

103
00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:00.413
Now pause the video.

104
00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:01.833
Open the terminal,

105
00:03:01.833 --> 00:03:03.493
type pwd, and write down the folder

106
00:03:03.493 --> 00:03:04.813
you get.

107
00:03:04.813 --> 00:03:07.240
Then type ls and write down three

108
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.080
names.

109
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.480
On the Mac you'll see names like

110
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.580
Documents.

111
00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.180
On a freshly installed Ubuntu,

112
00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.840
the list may be empty,

113
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:14.440
and that's normal.

114
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.400
If you see command not found,

115
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.560
check your typing:

116
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.980
all lowercase, no space in the middle.

117
00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.960
Now let's put Codex on the computer.

118
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:23.540
Codex is the program that will work

119
00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:24.900
inside the terminal,

120
00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.380
in a folder of yours.

121
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:27.580
First,

122
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.020
a distinction that avoids a lot of

123
00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.140
confusion:

124
00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:31.860
putting the program on the computer,

125
00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.300
signing in with your account,

126
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.700
and opening the program in the project

127
00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.080
folder are three different steps,

128
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.720
each with its own check.

129
00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.460
This lesson does only the first one.

130
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.060
The account comes in lesson fifteen,

131
00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.410
and the folder in lesson

132
00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.080
sixteen.

133
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.300
Right after installing,

134
00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.420
Lúcia typed codex and the program asked

135
00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.040
her to sign in with her account.

136
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.200
She thought it was a bug.

137
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.200
It wasn't.

138
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.740
The first step had worked,

139
00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:58.780
and the second was still missing.

140
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.400
When you separate the steps,

141
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.080
each new message stops looking like an

142
00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.280
error and starts telling you where you

143
00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.233
are along the way.

144
00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.060
Faster commands for installing programs circulate on

145
00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.360
the internet.

146
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.120
But a command pasted without checking can

147
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.500
run anything.

148
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:14.840
Think of a package that arrives at

149
00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.020
the school office:

150
00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.320
before opening it,

151
00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.080
you look at the seal and the

152
00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:19.720
sender.

153
00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.100
A command works the same way,

154
00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.160
and the sender is the address it

155
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.560
comes from.

156
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.100
The official Codex command downloads a script

157
00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.840
from the chatgpt.com

158
00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.560
address and runs that script.

159
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.340
That's why the address matters so much.

160
00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.970
Denise got a faster way to install

161
00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.440
Codex in a group chat.

162
00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:39.420
The address in the middle of the

163
00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:41.740
command had codex in its name,

164
00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:42.860
but it wasn't the one from the

165
00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.120
official page.

166
00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.840
She didn't paste it and used the

167
00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.220
one from the page.

168
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.120
So

169
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.820
the rule is simple:

170
00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.160
you don't paste a command from an

171
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.420
unknown page, and you read the address

172
00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.000
inside the command,

173
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.780
not just the title of the message.

174
00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.780
On macOS and Linux,

175
00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.040
the command from the official page starts

176
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.300
with curl, which downloads the file from

177
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.420
the address,

178
00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:05.740
and hands that file to sh,

179
00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.160
which runs it.

180
00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.169
On Windows with WSL,

181
00:05:09.169 --> 00:05:11.309
you paste the same command inside Ubuntu.

182
00:05:11.309 --> 00:05:13.189
The installer prints a few lines,

183
00:05:13.189 --> 00:05:14.729
and you don't need to understand each

184
00:05:14.729 --> 00:05:16.549
one: wait for the dollar sign to

185
00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:17.789
come back.

186
00:05:17.789 --> 00:05:19.809
Then ask for the version with the

187
00:05:19.809 --> 00:05:21.469
command codex dash dash version.

188
00:05:21.469 --> 00:05:23.309
If you see command not found,

189
00:05:23.309 --> 00:05:25.669
the terminal you opened earlier doesn't know

190
00:05:25.669 --> 00:05:27.169
about the new program yet.

191
00:05:27.169 --> 00:05:28.589
That's what happened to Denise:

192
00:05:28.589 --> 00:05:30.329
she closed the terminal,

193
00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:31.229
opened another one,

194
00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:32.809
and the number showed up.

195
00:05:32.809 --> 00:05:33.509
Pause the video.

196
00:05:33.509 --> 00:05:35.709
Open the official Codex page,

197
00:05:35.709 --> 00:05:37.809
check that the command is the same,

198
00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:39.729
paste it, wait,

199
00:05:39.729 --> 00:05:40.629
reopen the terminal,

200
00:05:40.629 --> 00:05:43.249
and write down the version number.

201
00:05:43.469 --> 00:05:45.429
If the computer belongs to the school

202
00:05:45.429 --> 00:05:47.629
and asks for a password,

203
00:05:47.629 --> 00:05:49.989
stop and talk to whoever manages it.

204
00:05:49.989 --> 00:05:51.529
Codex is on the computer,

205
00:05:51.529 --> 00:05:52.589
but

206
00:05:52.589 --> 00:05:53.949
it doesn't know who you are yet.

207
00:05:53.949 --> 00:05:56.129
At school, you swipe your badge at

208
00:05:56.129 --> 00:05:59.189
the turnstile and nobody hears your password.

209
00:05:59.189 --> 00:06:01.789
The Codex login works the same way.

210
00:06:01.789 --> 00:06:03.389
You type codex login,

211
00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:04.589
the

212
00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:06.409
terminal sends you to the browser,

213
00:06:06.409 --> 00:06:07.729
and that's where you sign in with

214
00:06:07.729 --> 00:06:09.329
your ChatGPT account.

215
00:06:09.329 --> 00:06:10.769
When you finish,

216
00:06:10.769 --> 00:06:12.859
Codex gets the confirmation and saves the

217
00:06:12.859 --> 00:06:13.379
sign-in.

218
00:06:13.379 --> 00:06:16.319
The password never goes through the terminal.

219
00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:17.979
Lúcia did exactly that:

220
00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:19.759
she typed codex login,

221
00:06:19.759 --> 00:06:22.039
the browser opened on the ChatGPT sign-in

222
00:06:22.039 --> 00:06:23.919
screen, she signed in with the school

223
00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:25.719
account, and went back to the terminal.

224
00:06:25.719 --> 00:06:28.279
One precaution before you start:

225
00:06:28.279 --> 00:06:32.199
which ChatGPT plans include Codex changes over

226
00:06:32.199 --> 00:06:33.219
time.

227
00:06:33.219 --> 00:06:35.339
Check the official authentication page to see

228
00:06:35.339 --> 00:06:36.139
if your plan is there.

229
00:06:36.139 --> 00:06:38.419
The plan name appears in your account

230
00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:38.999
settings.

231
00:06:38.999 --> 00:06:40.739
Being connected isn't enough.

232
00:06:40.739 --> 00:06:43.979
What matters is which path you took.

233
00:06:43.979 --> 00:06:46.259
Signing in with the account uses that

234
00:06:46.259 --> 00:06:47.259
account's plan.

235
00:06:47.259 --> 00:06:49.639
An API key is billed by usage,

236
00:06:49.639 --> 00:06:50.779
on a different account.

237
00:06:50.779 --> 00:06:52.559
It's that difference from lesson five:

238
00:06:52.559 --> 00:06:54.319
two accounts, two bills.

239
00:06:54.319 --> 00:06:56.139
To find out the path,

240
00:06:56.139 --> 00:06:58.939
use the command codex login status.

241
00:06:59.019 --> 00:06:59.819
With the account,

242
00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:00.879
the answer is:

243
00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:02.879
Logged in using ChatGPT.

244
00:07:02.879 --> 00:07:04.179
With a key,

245
00:07:04.179 --> 00:07:05.239
it mentions the key,

246
00:07:05.239 --> 00:07:06.899
but never the full value.

247
00:07:06.899 --> 00:07:08.839
Denise expected to see

248
00:07:08.839 --> 00:07:11.179
ChatGPT and found out Codex was signing

249
00:07:11.179 --> 00:07:13.059
in with an old school API key.

250
00:07:13.059 --> 00:07:15.488
She logged out with codex logout and

251
00:07:15.488 --> 00:07:17.788
signed in again with codex login,

252
00:07:17.788 --> 00:07:19.288
using the account.

253
00:07:19.528 --> 00:07:21.708
So read that answer before asking for

254
00:07:21.708 --> 00:07:23.028
any long task.

255
00:07:23.028 --> 00:07:25.208
It's what tells you which account the

256
00:07:25.208 --> 00:07:26.948
usage comes from.

257
00:07:26.948 --> 00:07:29.328
If you're signing in with your ChatGPT

258
00:07:29.328 --> 00:07:31.708
account, you can skip this part.

259
00:07:31.708 --> 00:07:33.428
People who use the API keep the

260
00:07:33.428 --> 00:07:36.048
key under a name the terminal knows,

261
00:07:36.448 --> 00:07:38.308
OPENAI API KEY,

262
00:07:38.308 --> 00:07:40.188
and the official command hands that value

263
00:07:40.188 --> 00:07:41.008
straight to Codex,

264
00:07:41.008 --> 00:07:42.208
without

265
00:07:42.208 --> 00:07:43.908
showing it on the screen.

266
00:07:43.908 --> 00:07:45.368
The common mistake is typing the whole

267
00:07:45.368 --> 00:07:46.968
key into the command:

268
00:07:46.968 --> 00:07:48.708
it stays in the history and shows

269
00:07:48.708 --> 00:07:50.488
up in any screenshot.

270
00:07:50.588 --> 00:07:52.108
And a dot env file on its

271
00:07:52.108 --> 00:07:54.108
own doesn't connect anything.

272
00:07:54.108 --> 00:07:55.868
When a colleague asked Lúcia for the

273
00:07:55.868 --> 00:07:57.848
school's key, she didn't send it through

274
00:07:57.848 --> 00:08:00.108
the group: each person signs in with

275
00:08:00.108 --> 00:08:01.708
their own account.

276
00:08:02.328 --> 00:08:03.568
Pause the video.

277
00:08:03.568 --> 00:08:04.968
Type codex login,

278
00:08:04.968 --> 00:08:06.528
sign in on the browser,

279
00:08:06.528 --> 00:08:08.628
and then run codex login status.

280
00:08:08.628 --> 00:08:10.368
On the project sheet,

281
00:08:10.468 --> 00:08:12.128
write a line like Denise's:

282
00:08:12.128 --> 00:08:13.808
Codex, ChatGPT,

283
00:08:13.808 --> 00:08:15.608
checked with codex login status.

284
00:08:15.608 --> 00:08:16.988
Make sure there's no password at all

285
00:08:16.988 --> 00:08:17.677
on that line.

286
00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.160
Codex is connected,

287
00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.060
but which folder will it work in?

288
00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:22.220
It works in the folder where it

289
00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:23.560
was opened.

290
00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:24.740
In the wrong folder,

291
00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:26.400
it reads what it shouldn't and can't

292
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:27.180
find what it needs.

293
00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.280
If you walk into the wrong room,

294
00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:30.180
you teach the wrong class.

295
00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:32.380
So you go into the folder before

296
00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.020
opening the program.

297
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:35.960
Three commands do this.

298
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.180
mkdir dash p creates the folder,

299
00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:39.140
and

300
00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:40.140
the ones before it too,

301
00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:41.600
if they're missing.

302
00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:43.600
cd goes into it.

303
00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:46.140
And pwd confirms the address.

304
00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.680
The tilde sign means my personal folder,

305
00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.440
the one with your name and the

306
00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.800
little house icon in the Mac Finder.

307
00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.880
Denise created the training folder,

308
00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.180
meu primeiro projeto,

309
00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.620
and went into it.

310
00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:00.200
The first two commands don't answer anything

311
00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:01.180
when they work.

312
00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:02.520
What confirmed the address,

313
00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.520
before she opened any program,

314
00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:05.580
was pwd.

315
00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.080
You don't need to open your entire

316
00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.780
personal folder to try this out.

317
00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:09.780
A small area,

318
00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.660
with only the material for the task,

319
00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:14.380
reduces confusion, and when something goes wrong,

320
00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:16.720
it's clear which files could have changed.

321
00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.660
Lúcia thought about opening Codex in her

322
00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.080
Documents folder, where her classes' tests and

323
00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:21.800
grades are.

324
00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:23.401
She chose the training folder instead,

325
00:09:23.401 --> 00:09:25.961
with a fictional agenda.

326
00:09:26.061 --> 00:09:28.401
Nothing real was within reach.

327
00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:29.981
Three things go inside it.

328
00:09:29.981 --> 00:09:32.381
The README describes the purpose of the

329
00:09:32.381 --> 00:09:34.581
project for whoever arrives.

330
00:09:34.581 --> 00:09:35.781
AGENTS.md

331
00:09:35.841 --> 00:09:38.921
gives the agent its working instructions.

332
00:09:39.201 --> 00:09:41.241
Both end in dot md because

333
00:09:41.241 --> 00:09:43.461
they're Markdown, plain text where the hash

334
00:09:43.461 --> 00:09:45.361
sign marks the title and the hyphen

335
00:09:45.361 --> 00:09:47.121
marks a list item.

336
00:09:47.121 --> 00:09:49.681
Denise put two rules in AGENTS.md:

337
00:09:49.681 --> 00:09:51.761
work only in that folder and don't

338
00:09:51.761 --> 00:09:53.221
send anything.

339
00:09:54.201 --> 00:09:56.761
And the entradas folder holds the fictional

340
00:09:56.761 --> 00:09:57.841
meeting agenda.

341
00:09:57.841 --> 00:09:59.621
Before typing codex,

342
00:09:59.621 --> 00:10:01.261
run pwd and ls.

343
00:10:01.261 --> 00:10:03.641
If the address and the files match,

344
00:10:03.641 --> 00:10:04.601
open the program there.

345
00:10:04.601 --> 00:10:06.501
The first time in a folder,

346
00:10:06.501 --> 00:10:09.261
Codex asks whether you trust it,

347
00:10:09.901 --> 00:10:11.561
because there it can read,

348
00:10:11.561 --> 00:10:12.761
edit, and run files,

349
00:10:12.761 --> 00:10:14.681
within your permissions.

350
00:10:14.721 --> 00:10:16.161
It's your training folder:

351
00:10:16.161 --> 00:10:18.341
choose Trust and continue with the arrow

352
00:10:18.341 --> 00:10:19.461
keys and press Enter.

353
00:10:19.461 --> 00:10:20.981
In this practice,

354
00:10:20.981 --> 00:10:22.701
don't use Open restricted.

355
00:10:22.701 --> 00:10:24.181
The wording may change a bit between

356
00:10:24.181 --> 00:10:25.381
versions.

357
00:10:25.521 --> 00:10:28.741
To exit, type slash quit.

358
00:10:28.741 --> 00:10:30.381
Now pause the video.

359
00:10:30.381 --> 00:10:33.283
Copy the practice block from the lesson,

360
00:10:33.283 --> 00:10:35.003
which creates the folder and the three

361
00:10:35.003 --> 00:10:36.223
fictional files,

362
00:10:36.223 --> 00:10:38.983
and paste it into the terminal.

363
00:10:38.983 --> 00:10:41.283
Check that pwd ends in meu primeiro

364
00:10:41.283 --> 00:10:44.303
projeto and that ls shows

365
00:10:45.183 --> 00:10:46.203
AGENTS.md,

366
00:10:46.203 --> 00:10:47.323
README.md,

367
00:10:47.323 --> 00:10:48.543
and entradas.

368
00:10:48.543 --> 00:10:50.263
Then type codex,

369
00:10:50.703 --> 00:10:51.403
trust the folder,

370
00:10:51.403 --> 00:10:54.543
and exit with slash quit,

371
00:10:54.543 --> 00:10:56.463
without asking for anything yet.

372
00:10:56.463 --> 00:10:58.483
Codex is in the right folder.

373
00:10:58.483 --> 00:11:00.523
What should the first request be?

374
00:11:00.523 --> 00:11:02.783
A request like fix up the project

375
00:11:02.783 --> 00:11:03.703
mixes diagnosis and change,

376
00:11:03.703 --> 00:11:04.643
and

377
00:11:04.643 --> 00:11:06.123
if something goes wrong,

378
00:11:06.123 --> 00:11:07.903
you don't know which part it was.

379
00:11:07.903 --> 00:11:10.623
No serious person starts a renovation by

380
00:11:10.623 --> 00:11:12.303
knocking down a wall.

381
00:11:12.303 --> 00:11:13.923
First comes the inspection:

382
00:11:13.923 --> 00:11:15.283
look, take notes,

383
00:11:15.283 --> 00:11:15.783
understand.

384
00:11:15.703 --> 00:11:19.203
With an agent it's the same order,

385
00:11:19.203 --> 00:11:20.743
in four steps.

386
00:11:20.743 --> 00:11:23.463
Inspect, by reading and explaining,

387
00:11:23.463 --> 00:11:25.563
without editing anything.

388
00:11:25.823 --> 00:11:28.103
Plan, by saying what's missing,

389
00:11:28.103 --> 00:11:30.443
and that list becomes your reference.

390
00:11:30.543 --> 00:11:31.823
Change a single file,

391
00:11:31.823 --> 00:11:33.623
by name, and you're the one who

392
00:11:33.623 --> 00:11:34.563
says which.

393
00:11:34.563 --> 00:11:35.703
And validate:

394
00:11:35.703 --> 00:11:37.004
you open it and check,

395
00:11:37.004 --> 00:11:39.444
because you have the final word.

396
00:11:39.724 --> 00:11:41.504
Denise was going to ask Codex to

397
00:11:41.504 --> 00:11:43.064
improve the meeting folder.

398
00:11:43.064 --> 00:11:44.844
She switched to two requests:

399
00:11:44.844 --> 00:11:47.144
first read and say what's missing,

400
00:11:47.144 --> 00:11:48.804
then create a single file.

401
00:11:48.804 --> 00:11:49.964
In the training folder,

402
00:11:49.964 --> 00:11:52.144
ask Codex to read AGENTS.md

403
00:11:54.304 --> 00:11:57.384
and the README and explain the purpose.

404
00:11:57.604 --> 00:11:58.264
Say it plainly:

405
00:11:58.264 --> 00:12:00.144
no editing.

406
00:12:00.284 --> 00:12:02.044
And ask it to name the files

407
00:12:02.044 --> 00:12:02.944
it used.

408
00:12:02.944 --> 00:12:04.604
Lúcia made that request,

409
00:12:04.604 --> 00:12:07.324
and Codex's actual answer was roughly this:

410
00:12:07.324 --> 00:12:09.804
the purpose is to prepare the agenda

411
00:12:09.804 --> 00:12:10.984
for a teachers' meeting.

412
00:12:10.984 --> 00:12:13.744
What's missing is the finished agenda,

413
00:12:13.744 --> 00:12:15.824
the output format and location,

414
00:12:15.824 --> 00:12:17.524
and how to check the result.

415
00:12:17.524 --> 00:12:19.724
And one important detail:

416
00:12:19.724 --> 00:12:21.764
the input file exists,

417
00:12:21.764 --> 00:12:23.384
but I didn't read its contents.

418
00:12:23.384 --> 00:12:24.304
Nothing

419
00:12:24.304 --> 00:12:25.404
was edited.

420
00:12:25.404 --> 00:12:27.624
Now compare the list of what it

421
00:12:27.624 --> 00:12:30.264
used with what ls shows in the

422
00:12:30.264 --> 00:12:30.764
folder.

423
00:12:30.504 --> 00:12:31.964
It didn't open the agenda,

424
00:12:31.964 --> 00:12:34.264
so the list of what's missing comes

425
00:12:34.264 --> 00:12:35.664
only from the README.

426
00:12:35.664 --> 00:12:37.144
Denise read that limit and,

427
00:12:37.144 --> 00:12:37.904
in the second request,

428
00:12:37.904 --> 00:12:39.764
made it clear that the plan had

429
00:12:39.764 --> 00:12:41.125
to be based on the agenda.

430
00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:43.420
Now authorize a small change:

431
00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:44.640
create only plano.md,

432
00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.780
with three actions and a check for

433
00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:48.180
each one,

434
00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.320
based on the agenda.

435
00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:51.880
Codex may ask whether you want to

436
00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:52.980
make the edits.

437
00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:55.340
If the change is only to plano.md,

438
00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:56.960
choose Yes, proceed.

439
00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:58.540
If it's another file,

440
00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:00.840
choose the option that starts with No.

441
00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:02.700
It may also create the file without

442
00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:05.420
asking, because the current permissions let it

443
00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:06.640
write in the folder;

444
00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:07.140
check with ls.

445
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:07.980
The

446
00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:10.220
plan Lúcia got covered the five items

447
00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.740
on the agenda and noted that times

448
00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:14.840
and dates still

449
00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:15.920
had to be set.

450
00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:17.800
She checked it against

451
00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:20.920
reuniao.txt, and everything was there.

452
00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:21.960
Pause the video.

453
00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:23.460
Make the reading request,

454
00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.920
then the plan request.

455
00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:27.220
Exit with slash quit,

456
00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.820
show the file with cat plano.md,

457
00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:31.020
and

458
00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.180
check one of the checks directly against

459
00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:34.360
the agenda.

460
00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:35.560
In the last lesson,

461
00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:37.340
the agent created a file.

462
00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:38.820
What if it had created the wrong

463
00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:41.160
one, or deleted another?

464
00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.520
Whoever is going to use the lab

465
00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:44.300
gets the key to the lab,

466
00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:45.911
not the whole key ring.

467
00:13:45.911 --> 00:13:47.731
With an agent it's the same:

468
00:13:47.731 --> 00:13:49.471
permissions control what it can reach in

469
00:13:49.471 --> 00:13:50.931
files, on the internet,

470
00:13:50.931 --> 00:13:52.731
and in commands.

471
00:13:52.731 --> 00:13:54.831
Go up one step at a time.

472
00:13:54.831 --> 00:13:56.891
Reading files is low risk,

473
00:13:57.111 --> 00:13:58.771
writing is medium risk,

474
00:13:58.771 --> 00:14:01.371
and running commands is high risk.

475
00:14:01.391 --> 00:14:03.151
And notice one difference.

476
00:14:03.331 --> 00:14:05.891
What you write in AGENTS.md guides the

477
00:14:05.891 --> 00:14:06.811
agent,

478
00:14:06.811 --> 00:14:09.011
but it's text and doesn't technically prevent

479
00:14:09.011 --> 00:14:09.511
anything.

480
00:14:09.431 --> 00:14:12.751
What prevents things is the program's permissions.

481
00:14:12.751 --> 00:14:15.051
In Codex, the slash permissions command shows

482
00:14:15.051 --> 00:14:17.031
and changes those permissions.

483
00:14:17.031 --> 00:14:19.131
When Codex asked for internet access to

484
00:14:19.131 --> 00:14:20.671
look up some documentation,

485
00:14:20.671 --> 00:14:22.851
Lúcia evaluated just that request,

486
00:14:22.851 --> 00:14:25.551
without granting anything else along with it.

487
00:14:25.611 --> 00:14:28.311
Going back requires having saved the before.

488
00:14:28.311 --> 00:14:30.971
The command cp dash r copies an

489
00:14:30.971 --> 00:14:33.651
entire folder, with everything inside.

490
00:14:34.111 --> 00:14:36.491
Make the copy before the change,

491
00:14:36.491 --> 00:14:38.231
with a name that says what it

492
00:14:38.231 --> 00:14:38.731
is.

493
00:14:38.471 --> 00:14:41.431
Before authorizing a change to AGENTS.md,

494
00:14:42.291 --> 00:14:44.291
Denise copied the training folder with the

495
00:14:44.291 --> 00:14:45.711
ending dash antes,

496
00:14:45.711 --> 00:14:47.451
which means before.

497
00:14:47.451 --> 00:14:50.271
cp doesn't answer anything when it works.

498
00:14:50.391 --> 00:14:52.241
What confirmed it was ls,

499
00:14:52.241 --> 00:14:54.281
with the two folders side by side.

500
00:14:54.281 --> 00:14:57.421
The folder ending in antes isn't touched

501
00:14:57.421 --> 00:14:58.581
by the agent:

502
00:14:58.581 --> 00:15:00.501
it's your return point.

503
00:15:00.541 --> 00:15:02.501
Later on, in module five,

504
00:15:02.501 --> 00:15:03.921
Git does this in a more complete

505
00:15:03.921 --> 00:15:04.981
way.

506
00:15:04.981 --> 00:15:06.501
For now, a simple copy does the

507
00:15:06.501 --> 00:15:07.001
job.

508
00:15:06.521 --> 00:15:08.581
And don't remove all of the program's

509
00:15:08.581 --> 00:15:10.401
protections to get around an error:

510
00:15:10.401 --> 00:15:13.361
start with the most restrictive setting that

511
00:15:13.361 --> 00:15:14.681
can handle the task.

512
00:15:14.681 --> 00:15:15.921
After the change,

513
00:15:15.921 --> 00:15:18.121
the command diff dash r compares the

514
00:15:18.121 --> 00:15:19.881
copy with the current folder and shows

515
00:15:19.881 --> 00:15:21.261
only the differences,

516
00:15:21.261 --> 00:15:22.501
file by file.

517
00:15:22.501 --> 00:15:24.361
The output looks like code,

518
00:15:24.361 --> 00:15:26.281
but you only need two things:

519
00:15:26.281 --> 00:15:27.221
which files show up,

520
00:15:27.221 --> 00:15:29.421
and which lines start with the greater-than

521
00:15:29.421 --> 00:15:31.661
sign, which are the new ones.

522
00:15:31.661 --> 00:15:32.561
In Lúcia's diff,

523
00:15:32.561 --> 00:15:34.741
a single file showed up,

524
00:15:34.741 --> 00:15:36.941
AGENTS.md, with two new lines.

525
00:15:36.941 --> 00:15:39.601
That was exactly what she had authorized.

526
00:15:39.621 --> 00:15:41.301
If another file shows up,

527
00:15:41.301 --> 00:15:42.741
the antes copy has the old version

528
00:15:42.741 --> 00:15:44.381
for you to restore.

529
00:15:44.581 --> 00:15:45.541
Pause the video.

530
00:15:45.541 --> 00:15:46.601
Make the copy,

531
00:15:46.601 --> 00:15:48.761
open Codex in the training folder,

532
00:15:48.761 --> 00:15:51.241
look at slash permissions without changing anything,

533
00:15:51.241 --> 00:15:53.161
and ask for two new lines in

534
00:15:53.161 --> 00:15:54.361
AGENTS.md,

535
00:15:55.241 --> 00:15:57.452
without changing anything else.

536
00:15:57.452 --> 00:15:59.212
Exit and run the diff.

537
00:15:59.212 --> 00:16:02.492
You're done when only AGENTS.md shows up,

538
00:16:02.492 --> 00:16:04.332
with the two new lines.

539
00:16:04.332 --> 00:16:05.872
Let's put it all together.

540
00:16:05.872 --> 00:16:08.292
You open the terminal and ask where

541
00:16:08.292 --> 00:16:09.792
you are with pwd,

542
00:16:09.792 --> 00:16:12.012
and what's there with ls.

543
00:16:12.012 --> 00:16:14.792
You install Codex from the official source,

544
00:16:14.792 --> 00:16:16.912
reading the address inside the command,

545
00:16:16.912 --> 00:16:18.672
and check it with the version.

546
00:16:18.672 --> 00:16:21.092
You sign in with your account through

547
00:16:21.092 --> 00:16:22.272
the browser,

548
00:16:22.272 --> 00:16:24.692
check the method with codex login status,

549
00:16:24.692 --> 00:16:26.732
and write only the method on the

550
00:16:26.732 --> 00:16:28.232
sheet, never the key.

551
00:16:28.232 --> 00:16:30.352
You open the agent in a small

552
00:16:30.352 --> 00:16:32.412
folder, with only the material for the

553
00:16:32.412 --> 00:16:32.912
task.

554
00:16:32.672 --> 00:16:34.792
You ask first for a reading,

555
00:16:34.792 --> 00:16:36.012
then for a change by name,

556
00:16:36.012 --> 00:16:37.212
and

557
00:16:37.272 --> 00:16:38.972
check it against the real material.

558
00:16:38.972 --> 00:16:40.972
And before changing anything,

559
00:16:40.972 --> 00:16:42.862
you save a copy and compare with

560
00:16:42.862 --> 00:16:43.362
diff.

561
00:16:42.972 --> 00:16:44.972
When you have about thirty minutes,

562
00:16:44.972 --> 00:16:46.212
do the module lab,

563
00:16:46.212 --> 00:16:48.112
First guided project,

564
00:16:48.112 --> 00:16:50.692
in the supplementary material for lesson eighteen.

565
00:16:50.692 --> 00:16:52.232
In the next module,

566
00:16:52.232 --> 00:16:53.792
Folders, Markdown, and secrets:

567
00:16:53.792 --> 00:16:55.792
the project's digital home,

568
00:16:55.792 --> 00:16:57.732
with instruction files and a separate place

569
00:16:57.732 --> 00:16:59.092
for passwords.

570
00:16:59.692 --> 00:17:00.727
See you there.
